# deploy/Caddyfile
#
# Recommended production topology:
#
#   Internet --(HTTPS)--> Caddy (this file, TLS via Let's Encrypt) --(HTTP,
#   localhost only)--> WAF proxy (gunicorn, config/waf_config.yaml) -->
#   your real backend
#
# Why Caddy in front instead of the WAF's own built-in TLS mode
# (config/waf_config.yaml tls.enabled): Caddy automatically obtains and
# renews a real Let's Encrypt certificate with zero extra tooling, handles
# HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, and is a single, well-audited binary whose only job is
# being the internet-facing edge. Reinventing ACME/cert renewal inside the
# WAF itself would be a lot of extra surface area for something Caddy
# already does correctly. The WAF's own tls.enabled option still exists
# for standalone deployments where you don't want a second process, or
# already have a cert from elsewhere.
#
# Install Caddy on Arch:
#   sudo pacman -S caddy
# Then:
#   sudo cp deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   # edit the domain name below first!
#   sudo systemctl enable --now caddy

your-domain.com {
    # Caddy gets a real cert for this domain automatically (make sure
    # your-domain.com's DNS A/AAAA record already points at this server,
    # and that ports 80+443 are reachable from the internet -- Caddy needs
    # port 80 briefly during the ACME HTTP-01 challenge).

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8443 {
        # Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For automatically. The WAF only trusts
        # this header from IPs listed in config/waf_config.yaml's
        # trusted_proxies -- make sure 127.0.0.1 is in that list, since
        # that's what Caddy connects from here.
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
    }

    # Optional: Caddy's own access log, separate from the WAF's structured
    # JSON log -- useful for raw edge-level traffic analysis.
    log {
        output file /var/log/caddy/access.log
    }
}
